WEBVTT

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:07.867
A transceiver sits at the very edge of the board, where miswiring and static strike first.

00:00:07.867 --> 00:00:12.565
SKU-5 puts two bus links on one die, built for that edge.

00:00:13.615 --> 00:00:16.300
The core logic is only a thin skin.

00:00:16.300 --> 00:00:23.282
Everything that decides how the part behaves on the wire is analog, on thick-oxide devices.

00:00:24.332 --> 00:00:29.306
How far a signal travels is set by how its edges rise and fall.

00:00:29.306 --> 00:00:33.649
So every edge is shaped, and every delay accounted for.

00:00:34.699 --> 00:00:39.765
The outputs drive a half-duplex bus with up to 256 nodes.

00:00:39.765 --> 00:00:47.231
Fast over a short run, or slow across 1.2 kilometres: two operating points, not one.

00:00:48.281 --> 00:00:59.225
The receiver reads the bus back with hysteresis, and a dead or open bus reads as a known state, never as noise the logic could take for data.

00:01:00.275 --> 00:01:08.296
The second channel repeats the same path for CAN-FD, with its own supply and clock recovery beside it.

00:01:09.346 --> 00:01:13.310
Each pin that leaves the board brings its own protection.

00:01:13.310 --> 00:01:16.648
A short, or overheating, shuts its channel down.

00:01:17.698 --> 00:01:19.607
SKU-5 is pre-silicon.

00:01:19.607 --> 00:01:25.699
Every figure is a target, and ESD survival has no silicon data yet.

00:01:25.699 --> 00:01:30.972
Bring us your cable, your node count and your fault cases.

00:01:30.972 --> 00:01:34.518
We will tell you what SKU-5 must prove.
